مرحلة 31 — النقاط والشارات للطلاب
PHASE 31: POINTS AND BADGES
Flag: gamification. Off by default per center.
- Rules editor: points for attendance, an attendance streak, perfect month, on-time assignment, exam score thresholds, and manual teacher awards with a reason. Badge definitions with Arabic names, simple icons (no emoji) and criteria. Levels based on cumulative points.
- Student portal: points, current level progress, badges earned, and recent activity. Parent portal: read-only view.
- Leaderboards are opt-in per group and privacy-minded: show only the top entries by first name or initials, never show the bottom of the ranking, and let teachers disable them. Prefer personal-progress and streak views over ranking.
- Rewards catalog defined by the center (for example a discount or a free note) with redemption requests approved by staff and tracked.
- Anti-gaming: points computed from verified events only, no double-awarding for the same event, and a staff reversal action with a reason and log entry.
Acceptance: awarding is idempotent per event; reversals recalculate levels; leaderboards respect the opt-in and privacy settings.
مرحلة 32 — مميزات الذكاء الاصطناعي
PHASE 32: AI ASSISTANT FEATURES
Flag: aiAssistant (top plan). Master switch plus per-feature switches in Settings > الذكاء الاصطناعي, OFF by default.
Architecture: an AIProvider interface (generate(prompt, options)). Never put an API key in the browser; the real provider must be a server function (document the contract). Prototype uses a MockAIProvider returning realistic sample output. Per-center monthly quota, usage log (feature, tokens or calls, date), and a clear message when the quota ends. Privacy: send the minimum data, pseudonymize student names before sending, and let the owner opt in explicitly with an Arabic explanation of what is sent.

A) Exam question generation
- The teacher pastes lesson text (or picks a lesson's attached text), chooses question types, count and difficulty, and gets DRAFT questions with correct answers and explanations. Drafts land in a review queue where the teacher edits, approves or rejects each question. Nothing is ever published to students automatically, and approved questions are marked with their origin.

B) Early warning for dropout risk
- Start with an explainable rules-based score: consecutive or frequent absences, falling scores trend, overdue payments, missed assignments, low platform engagement. Each at-risk student shows a risk level (منخفض / متوسط / مرتفع) and the exact reasons. Weights are editable. Weekly list on the dashboard with suggested follow-up actions and a way to log the action taken and dismiss. Do not label students publicly and never show risk to the student; parents see nothing automatically.

C) Parent message drafting
- From student data the staff member picks a purpose (absence follow-up, praise, concern about scores, payment reminder) and a tone (formal or friendly Egyptian Arabic) and receives an editable draft. It is a suggestion only: the staff member edits and sends it through the normal messaging flow. Never auto-send.
Acceptance: with the flag off nothing AI-related is visible; drafts never reach students without teacher approval; usage is logged and capped; no secret is in the browser.
